fact WorkItem(id: "WI-865", description: some(value: "WI-857 FOLLOW-UP — a recorded-absent dictionary slot DISCARDS THE FAILURE KIND, so a provider TIE is reported as 'no provider'. WI-857 made an unresolvable SPEC-half slot carry an empty bundle over the anthill.reflect.NoProvider marker (ResolvedRequiresNode::Unavailable), refused at any USE by resolve_op_target_checked. The variant is built for NoMatch, Ambiguous and Cyclic ALIKE — deliberately, so the placement rule has no cases to get wrong — but the marker then carries nothing to runtime, so the refusal message can only say 'nothing provides it there, or more than one does' and can never say WHICH, nor name the bindings the message claims. That is a REGRESSION IN ATTRIBUTION relative to WI-843, which went to specific trouble to FORWARD a sub-goal tie verbatim rather than restamp it (resolve_at_goal: 'the tie is FORWARDED, not restamped'): the same tie is a precise coherence diagnostic at the call's own goal and an anonymous one inside a spec half. FIX DIRECTION (settle at pickup): carry the reason to runtime. The typer side is cheap — Unavailable { spec_sort, why } holding the discriminant or the InstanceTie. The RUNTIME side is the real work and the reason this is its own ticket: RequirementArena's Slot carries only functor: Option<Symbol>, so a marker can hold no payload. An explicit enum SlotFunctor { Provider(Symbol), NoProvider { spec: Symbol, why } } would (a) make 'get the provider' FALLIBLE AT ONE ACCESSOR, which forces every read site to handle it — today that discipline is held by resolve_op_target_checked plus a hand-written check in start_apply_deferred's proj_path loop; (b) let the slot carry the spec the diagnostic needs; (c) retire the sentinel-functor encoding, so 'unnameable marker' stops depending on SymbolTable::intern and define keeping disjoint maps. NOTE the typer IR needs a functor symbol in a construct_requirement TERM regardless, so the slot enum covers only the runtime half — the term half stays a sentinel unless Expr::ConstructRequirement grows a field. ACCEPTANCE: a two-provider tie inside a spec half reports as a TIE naming both candidates, not as 'no provider'; the existing wi857_dictionary_layout_test 'reading_an_unprovided_spec_half_slot_is_loud' still passes with its no-provider wording; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-17T10:18:20Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-865", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED 2026-08-17. SETTLED AT PICKUP, and the ticket's FIX DIRECTION was stale: WI-1045 retired `RequirementArena`, so there is no `Slot` to grow a `SlotFunctor` enum on — a dictionary is an ordinary `Value::Entity` (058 §9, one shape). Both ways to put the reason on that value break §9 (extra named children put keys on `Dictionary` that its sort does not declare and that the canonical named-arg order must then place; payload-carrying positional children make 'the children are sub-dictionaries' false in meaning while true in form), so the reason rides a KB SIDE TABLE keyed by the marker symbol (`KnowledgeBase::absence_records`, plus a back-index so a re-mint is one probe — `dictionary_of_tree` runs per call and WI-869 strictness puts a marker in every non-strict slot). The marker became a FAMILY: `absence_marker_sym` renders the name FROM the record, so distinct absences get distinct symbols, the name is a pure function of the record (idempotent re-mint, both producers agree), and `is_absence_marker` recognizes by PREFIX. Ticket item (c) — retire the sentinel — is DECLINED with a reason at the site: interned-never-DEFINED is what keeps `sort_ops_lookup` from answering, and a declared stdlib sort would be one `resolve_op_target` falls through on, which is the silent host-default dispatch the marker exists to refuse. FIVE KINDS, five sentences, one shared head ('pins no provider') that existing readers key on: NoProvider / Ambiguous (naming the tied candidates — the acceptance) / Cyclic / WI-869 NotThisDispatch / HostEntry. THREE THINGS MEASUREMENT FORCED, none anticipated. (1) A tie inside a spec half is often NOT the slot's own: `resolve_inner` returns a provider-half sub-goal's failure VERBATIM, so rendering against the SLOT's spec says 'more than one provider matched Base — MidA, MidB' when neither provides Base — a definite falsehood, WORSE than the hedge it replaced. So `ResolutionResult::NoMatch`/`Cyclic` gained `spec` (`Ambiguous` had it since WI-843 via `InstanceTie::spec`) and every arm renders against the FAILING level. (2) The SPEC is not the coordinate that separates the two levels: `provides Base[T = Wrap[E]]` beside `requires Base[T = Bool]` fails one level down on the SAME spec, so a bit rides instead — `forwarded` set at the ONE verbatim-return site, recorded as `AbsenceRecord::Slot::below`. (3) `below` decides WHOSE BINDINGS the sentence asserts, not just whether to name the slot. Bindings and `hint` are deliberately NOT carried: a String in the record would key the marker name, and a fresh-variable rendering differing per call would mint an unbounded symbol family on the per-call dispatch path. (1)-(3) were each found by /code-review, in three successive cuts, and each was DRIVEN before being fixed. Tests: `wi865_absence_reason_test` (9), one program shape under seven provider blocks, with five measured back-out axes; wi857's node assertion now pins `why` and `below`; wi869's sibling-evidence refusal now pins the NotThisDispatch attribution. Full workspace green (29 binaries, 4994 passed).", at: "2026-08-17T10:18:14Z")
